course: physics
lesson: 40
title: Seasons and tides
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Earth is tilted, so as it travels round the Sun each year, first one half and then the other leans towards the Sun; the half that leans in gets high, direct sunshine and long days, and that is summer.
explain: Shine a torch straight down on the table and the spot is small and bright; tilt it and the spot spreads out bigger and dimmer, and that is why a low winter Sun warms so much less.
explain: Seasons are not caused by the Earth getting closer to the Sun: the Earth is actually nearest to the Sun in January, in the northern winter, and Australians eat Christmas lunch on the beach.
explain: The Moon's pull raises the oceans into a bulge on the side facing it and another on the far side, and as the Earth spins each beach passes through both bulges, so the sea comes in and goes out twice a day.
explain: On a flat beach the tide can come back in faster than you can walk, so always check the tide times before you explore a sandbank.
fact: The Earth is tilted, so for half the year your half leans towards the Sun, gets more direct sunlight and longer days, and has summer.
fact: Seasons are not caused by distance from the Sun; the Earth is nearest to the Sun in January.
fact: The two halves of the Earth have opposite seasons, so Christmas comes in summer in Australia.
fact: In summer the Sun climbs high and takes a long path across the sky; near the North Pole it does not set at all in midsummer.
fact: A high Sun puts its light on a small patch and a low Sun spreads the same light over a big patch, so a low Sun warms less.
fact: Near the equator the Sun is high all year, so there is no snowy winter; many places there have wet and dry seasons instead.
fact: The Moon's gravity pulls the oceans into a bulge on the near side and another on the far side, and each place passes through both as the Earth spins, giving two high tides a day.
fact: Tides come about 50 minutes later each day, because the Moon moves on in its orbit.
fact: When the Sun and Moon pull in line, at full and new Moon, tides are biggest; these are called spring tides, though they have nothing to do with spring.
fact: On some flat beaches the sea comes back in faster than you can walk; if cut off, call the coastguard rather than try to wade.
fact: The Moon pulls lakes too, but a lake is too small to show a tide you can see; even the solid ground rises and falls a few centimetres each day.
fact: A year is the time the Earth takes to go round the Sun, about 365 and a quarter days, so every fourth year has an extra day, 29 February, to keep the seasons in place on the calendar.
exercise: What causes summer and winter? | The Earth's tilt, so each half leans towards or away from the Sun | The Earth getting closer to and further from the Sun through the year | The Sun getting hotter and cooler through the year | Clouds blocking the Sun in winter
exercise: When is the Earth nearest to the Sun? | In January | In July | In March | It is always the same distance
exercise: Why is Christmas in summer in Australia? | The southern half leans towards the Sun when the northern half leans away | Australia is closer to the Sun than Britain | Australia moves its Christmas to summer | The Sun goes round Australia twice
exercise: Why are summer days longer than winter days? | The Sun climbs high and takes a long path across the sky | The Earth spins more slowly in summer | The Sun gives off more light in summer | Clocks are changed in spring to make the days last longer
exercise: Where can the Sun shine at midnight? | Near the North Pole in midsummer | At the equator on every single day | In any big city in the middle of winter | Nowhere on Earth, the Sun always sets
exercise: Why does a low Sun warm less than a high Sun? | Its light is spread over a bigger patch | Its light has to travel round the Earth | It is further from the Earth in the morning | It gives out less heat when it is low
exercise: Why don't countries near the equator have snowy winters? | The Sun is high there all year | They are closer to the Sun | The sea keeps them warm | Snow cannot fall on hot ground
exercise: What seasons do many countries near the equator have instead of summer and winter? | Wet and dry seasons | Hot and very hot seasons | Spring and autumn only | Day and night seasons
exercise: What pulls the oceans to make the tides? | The Moon's gravity, helped by the Sun | The wind blowing hard over the open sea | Big fish swimming together | The Earth's magnetism
exercise: Why are there two high tides a day? | The Moon raises bulges on both sides of the Earth, and each place passes through both | The Moon goes round the Earth twice a day | The Sun and the Moon each make one | Rivers fill the sea twice a day
exercise: Why do tides come about 50 minutes later each day? | The Moon moves on in its orbit | The Earth slows down every day | The Sun pushes the tides back | Clocks are slightly wrong
exercise: When are tides biggest? | When the Sun and Moon pull in line, at full and new Moon | Only in the spring months | When the wind is strongest | When the Moon is half full
exercise: What are spring tides? | The biggest tides, when Sun and Moon pull in line | Tides that only happen in spring | Tides that bounce like springs | Tides that come from the springs of water up in the hills
exercise: What should you do if you are cut off by the tide on a sandbank? | Call the coastguard rather than try to wade | Try to wade or swim back quickly before it gets deeper | Dig a hole and wait in it | Walk further out to sea
exercise: Why doesn't a lake have tides you can see? | It is too small for the Moon's pull to make a noticeable bulge | The Moon's pull cannot reach fresh water, only salty sea water | Lakes are too deep for tides | Trees around the lake block the Moon
exercise: Why do we have leap years? | The year is about 365 and a quarter days, and the quarters add up | The Earth speeds up every four years | The Moon needs an extra day every four years | To give people an extra holiday
test: my cousin reckons summer's hot cos we're closer to the sun then. right? | No, it's the tilt; the Earth is actually nearest in January | Yes, we're much closer in July | Yes, but only in Britain | No, summer is caused by the Moon coming closer and warming us
test: Shine a torch straight down and then at a slant. What does it show about winter sunshine? | Slanted light spreads over a bigger patch and warms less | Slanted light is hotter than straight light, so winters are warm | The torch gets dimmer when tilted | Light only shines straight down
test: An Australian pen pal says she's going to the beach on Christmas Day. Why isn't that a joke? | It is summer there in December | Australia has no winter at all | Australians move Christmas to July | The beach is heated for Christmas
test: In a cartoon, a lake in the park has high and low tides like the sea. What's wrong? | A lake is far too small to show tides you can see | Nothing, all water has big tides | Lakes have bigger tides than the sea | Lakes have tides only at night, when the Moon is out
test: oi, the tide's coming in round the sandbank, let's just wade back, it's shallow. good idea? | No, on flat beaches it comes in fast; call the coastguard if cut off | Yes, shallow water is always safe | Yes, if you run | Yes, tides always stop at your knees
test: What would happen to the calendar if there were no leap years? | The seasons would slowly drift through the months | Nothing at all would change | Every year would be a day shorter, and summer would vanish | The Moon would change its shape
test: Today the high tide was at 9 in the morning. About when will it be tomorrow? | About 50 minutes later | At exactly the same time | About two hours earlier | At midnight, when tides peak
test: Why might a fisherman want to know when the full Moon is? | Tides are biggest at full and new Moon | Fish only ever bite when the Moon is full | The full Moon warms up the sea for the fish | Boats float higher and better at full Moon
test: What would happen to seasons if the Earth were not tilted at all? | There would be no real summer and winter | Summer would last all year long everywhere | Winter would last all year long everywhere | The seasons would be twice as strong as now
test: Someone in Norway says she had sunshine at midnight in June. Could that be true? | Yes, near the North Pole the Sun does not set in midsummer | No, the Sun always sets at night | No, it was the Moon shining | Yes, but only on a cloudy day
test: Why does midday sunshine feel weaker in winter than in summer? | The Sun is lower, so its light is spread thinner | The Sun gives out less heat in winter | The Earth is further from the Sun in winter, so it is weaker | The cold air blocks sunlight
exam: Identify the principal cause of the seasons upon the Earth. | The inclination of the Earth's axis | The variation in the distance between the Earth and the Sun | The variation in the heat emitted by the Sun | The interposition of clouds during the winter months
exam: Ascertain the reason for which two high tides occur at a given location within a single day. | The Moon raises bulges of water on both the near and the far side of the Earth | The Moon completes two revolutions about the Earth each day | Rivers discharge into the sea on two occasions each day | The winds reverse their direction twice daily
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises spring tides? | Tides of the greatest range, occurring when the Sun and Moon act in line | Tides occurring exclusively during the spring months, when the snows melt | Tides of the least range, occurring at half Moon | Tides originating from springs of fresh water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the institution of leap years. | An additional day is inserted every fourth year to account for the excess quarter day | An additional day is inserted because the Earth accelerates periodically | An additional day is inserted to accommodate the Moon's orbit | An additional day is inserted solely by custom and for no astronomical reason
